414
00:17:51,810 --> 00:17:54,780
So, before I was at Berkeley, I worked at Ford

415
00:17:54,780 --> 00:17:57,063
then I got my PhD out in Michigan.

416
00:17:57,990 --> 00:18:00,540
As you can tell by my accent, I am a New Yorker originally,

417
00:18:00,540 --> 00:18:02,323
so please don't hold that against me.

418
00:18:02,323 --> 00:18:03,720
(audience titters)

419
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:07,110
So, Henry Ford supposedly said, almost certainly though,

420
00:18:07,110 --> 00:18:09,390
it's apocryphal, "If I had asked people

421
00:18:09,390 --> 00:18:12,960
for what they wanted, they would've said faster horses."

422
00:18:12,960 --> 00:18:15,840
This is true everywhere in new technology,

423
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:18,480
and you guys are all familiar with this.

424
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:20,340
As a general rule, the more revolutionary

425
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:22,143
and different the technology is,

426
00:18:23,310 --> 00:18:25,170
the least like the precedent will appear.

427
00:18:25,170 --> 00:18:27,660
And people are always wrong

428
00:18:27,660 --> 00:18:29,490
with what it's going to look like.

429
00:18:29,490 --> 00:18:33,150
Oftentimes the new technology is especially bad

430
00:18:33,150 --> 00:18:34,500
at what it's replacing.

431
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:35,520
Think of a cell phone.

432
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:38,370
The original iPhone was great at a lot of things,

433
00:18:38,370 --> 00:18:40,860
but it was absolutely miserable at making phone calls.

434
00:18:40,860 --> 00:18:43,100
The actual flip phones were much better at that.

435
00:18:43,100 --> 00:18:44,490
(mic humming)

436
00:18:44,490 --> 00:18:47,430
We are at that sort of inflection point now,

437
00:18:47,430 --> 00:18:49,950
especially with automated vehicles.

438
00:18:49,950 --> 00:18:52,500
We're further along with electric vehicles,

439
00:18:52,500 --> 00:18:54,750
but things are still changing.

440
00:18:54,750 --> 00:18:57,240
If you want to go back to the origins of the car,

441
00:18:57,240 --> 00:18:59,910
the very first cars were called horseless carriages

442
00:18:59,910 --> 00:19:02,700
and they looked like horseless carriages.

443
00:19:02,700 --> 00:19:05,490
The very first EVs were basically ICE cars

444
00:19:05,490 --> 00:19:07,530
that they just put a battery in.

445
00:19:07,530 --> 00:19:09,210
Didn't really take advantage

446
00:19:09,210 --> 00:19:14,190
of the unique benefits of electrification.

447
00:19:14,190 --> 00:19:17,070
We're seeing some small cool stuff like in the Rivian,

448
00:19:17,070 --> 00:19:19,249
you have that whatchamacallit?

449
00:19:19,249 --> 00:19:22,320
The throughput where the- - The gear tunnel.

450
00:19:22,320 --> 00:19:23,852
- What? - Gear tunnel.

451
00:19:23,852 --> 00:19:24,750
- The? - Gear tunnel.

452
00:19:24,750 --> 00:19:27,810
- The gear tunnel where, oh, it looks totally different.

453
00:19:27,810 --> 00:19:29,280
So, we have more storage space.

454
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:30,630
All these cars have fronts.

455
00:19:31,950 --> 00:19:33,900
But one of the biggest things I want to really stress

456
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:37,410
with you guys is that people love

457
00:19:37,410 --> 00:19:39,000
to predict 50 years in the future.

458
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,150
It's super easy to do that, especially with automation.

459
00:19:42,150 --> 00:19:43,830
We have a joke in the field

460
00:19:43,830 --> 00:19:46,530
that automated vehicles are only 10 years away

461
00:19:46,530 --> 00:19:49,350
and it's been that way for the last 75 years.

462
00:19:49,350 --> 00:19:52,020
I am not exaggerating that number.

463
00:19:52,020 --> 00:19:55,470
If anything, it's understating it.

464
00:19:55,470 --> 00:19:57,540
You first had automated vehicles pitched

465
00:19:57,540 --> 00:20:01,350
as a solution at the 1939 World's Fair

466
00:20:01,350 --> 00:20:03,540
by GM's Futurama exhibit.

467
00:20:03,540 --> 00:20:05,880
This is a more recent picture from GM.

468
00:20:05,880 --> 00:20:06,713
This is what automated is,

469
00:20:06,713 --> 00:20:08,460
this is what they thought automated vehicles

470
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:10,920
would look like in 1950.

471
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:12,510
Now, we have automated,

472
00:20:12,510 --> 00:20:14,310
thanks to companies like Nuro, Waymo, et cetera,

473
00:20:14,310 --> 00:20:17,250
we do have automated vehicles on the road today.

474
00:20:17,250 --> 00:20:20,760
But what they will look like, how they will be used,

475
00:20:20,760 --> 00:20:23,190
we don't know.

476
00:20:23,190 --> 00:20:24,930
This creates problems

477
00:20:24,930 --> 00:20:27,780
and challenges from a workforce development perspective

478
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:30,870
because it's not like I can say, "Oh great, we need

479
00:20:30,870 --> 00:20:33,420
to be training workers for AVs.

480
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:35,160
They need to learn A, B and C."

481
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:38,550
It's like, well, what type of AVs are we talking about?

482
00:20:38,550 --> 00:20:40,200
Are we trying to provide workers

483
00:20:40,200 --> 00:20:44,820
for Waymo-type solutions, which would be fully owned

484
00:20:44,820 --> 00:20:48,180
and operated, self-driving fleets of taxis at an L4?

485
00:20:48,180 --> 00:20:49,680
That's fully autonomous level.

486
00:20:49,680 --> 00:20:54,450
Or are we talking about supporting the OEMs in building

487
00:20:54,450 --> 00:20:56,370
more ADAS-like technologies?

488
00:20:56,370 --> 00:20:59,010
Those would be driver-assist technologies,

489
00:20:59,010 --> 00:21:01,170
including the, perhaps inappropriately named,

490
00:21:01,170 --> 00:21:03,033
full self-driving supervised.

491
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:05,419
Thank you.

492
00:21:05,419 --> 00:21:06,252
(audience chuckles)

493
00:21:06,252 --> 00:21:07,085
So,

494
00:21:07,085 --> 00:21:07,918
(mic humming)

495
00:21:07,918 --> 00:21:10,530
this is why we are hoping

496
00:21:10,530 --> 00:21:13,590
to help play a role in bringing folks together

497
00:21:13,590 --> 00:21:16,380
and learning together

498
00:21:16,380 --> 00:21:19,410
and trying to train the next generation of people,

499
00:21:19,410 --> 00:21:22,260
while keeping in mind just how uncertain this space is.

500
00:21:22,260 --> 00:21:24,900
Now, as I'm going through, I want you

501
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:26,190
to think about two main things.

502
00:21:26,190 --> 00:21:27,480
One,

503
00:21:27,480 --> 00:21:32,480
is, what is the relationship between the driver

504
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:35,760
and the vehicle when it's an autonomous vehicle?

505
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:37,500
So, this works in two different ways.

506
00:21:37,500 --> 00:21:38,970
One, and we're seeing this with a lot

507
00:21:38,970 --> 00:21:40,320
of the AV companies now,

508
00:21:40,320 --> 00:21:43,800
what does a higher driver do besides driving?

509
00:21:43,800 --> 00:21:45,900
It turns out, quite a lot.

510
00:21:45,900 --> 00:21:48,270
So, the famous line with Waymo's is they're driverless.

511
00:21:48,270 --> 00:21:50,700
They are very, very far from laborless.

512
00:21:50,700 --> 00:21:51,870
There is a lot of work

513
00:21:51,870 --> 00:21:53,040
and energy that needs

514
00:21:53,040 --> 00:21:55,140
to go into making a Waymo drive itself.

515
00:21:55,140 --> 00:21:58,020
And even in day-to-day operations, somebody needs

516
00:21:58,020 --> 00:22:00,900
to plug it into a charger, somebody needs to clean it.

517
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:02,500
There's a lot that goes into it.

518
00:22:03,720 --> 00:22:05,610
There is a flip side to that question though,

519
00:22:05,610 --> 00:22:09,390
is, why the heck are we automating this in the first place?

520
00:22:09,390 --> 00:22:13,380
Automation tends to be a very expensive

521
00:22:13,380 --> 00:22:15,840
and hardware-intensive technology,

522
00:22:15,840 --> 00:22:19,140
especially for the full automation level four,

523
00:22:19,140 --> 00:22:21,330
an obscene number of sensors

524
00:22:21,330 --> 00:22:23,670
and an obscene number of incredibly smart people,

525
00:22:23,670 --> 00:22:24,930
like my friend here,

526
00:22:24,930 --> 00:22:26,190
who have to work on this stuff.

527
00:22:26,190 --> 00:22:29,490
It is not cheap to make a vehicle drive itself,

528
00:22:29,490 --> 00:22:32,370
especially now, can cost come down in the future?

529
00:22:32,370 --> 00:22:35,730
Of course, but it's coming down from a high point.

530
00:22:35,730 --> 00:22:39,390
And I want to say this in as nice way as possible

531
00:22:39,390 --> 00:22:41,760
'cause I'm trying to keep politics out of it,

532
00:22:41,760 --> 00:22:45,690
but, traditionally, drivers have been one

533
00:22:45,690 --> 00:22:48,450
of the more abused segments of the population.

534
00:22:48,450 --> 00:22:51,180
They tend to be paid pretty gosh-darn poorly

535
00:22:51,180 --> 00:22:55,440
with a very few exceptions, with very few labor protections.

536
00:22:55,440 --> 00:22:59,070
Taxi drivers, Uber drivers being a classic example of that.

537
00:22:59,070 --> 00:23:02,850
So, you're trying to use a really expensive technology

538
00:23:02,850 --> 00:23:04,380
on a one-to-one basis

539
00:23:04,380 --> 00:23:07,950
to replace what is a very lowly paid worker.

540
00:23:07,950 --> 00:23:09,840
It's the same reason, you can make an argument that,

541
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:12,150
well, it's the same reason why McDonald's

542
00:23:12,150 --> 00:23:13,800
still hires a lot of people.

543
00:23:13,800 --> 00:23:16,890
It's not that you couldn't build a robot to flip burgers.

544
00:23:16,890 --> 00:23:20,460
It's that it turns out the person to flip the burgers

545
00:23:20,460 --> 00:23:22,650
is more effective and efficient and cheaper

546
00:23:22,650 --> 00:23:24,720
than building a robot to do it.

547
00:23:24,720 --> 00:23:27,240
So, whenever anyone says, "Oh, we can just automate it,"

548
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:31,260
the first question you need to ask is, could we do this

549
00:23:31,260 --> 00:23:32,280
with a human driver?

550
00:23:32,280 --> 00:23:34,950
And if not, why haven't we done it already?

551
00:23:34,950 --> 00:23:37,980
Now, that gets me to my second question is,

552
00:23:37,980 --> 00:23:41,520
is this a genuinely new task, a new technology?

553
00:23:41,520 --> 00:23:45,750
Is this vehicle operating in a fundamentally different way

554
00:23:45,750 --> 00:23:47,250
than it did before?

555
00:23:47,250 --> 00:23:49,320
And does that mean that we are gonna need new labor

556
00:23:49,320 --> 00:23:52,620
and support, again, with an AV that I was just describing?

557
00:23:52,620 --> 00:23:53,790
Yeah, it doesn't have a driver.

558
00:23:53,790 --> 00:23:56,760
So, we need to have a whole other set of folks who need

559
00:23:56,760 --> 00:23:59,010
to be involved with working with the sensors,

560
00:23:59,010 --> 00:24:01,620
calibrating the sensors, cleaning the vehicles, checking in.

561
00:24:01,620 --> 00:24:04,980
This is a new job that is absolutely required

562
00:24:04,980 --> 00:24:06,060
because of automation.

563
00:24:06,060 --> 00:24:08,260
There are many more beyond that, by the way.

564
00:24:09,180 --> 00:24:11,070
I'm mentioning this to you guys,

565
00:24:11,070 --> 00:24:13,200
and again, I'm trying not to get too political here,

566
00:24:13,200 --> 00:24:16,140
but it is a risk with any new technology

567
00:24:16,140 --> 00:24:19,230
that it's not actually the technology that is...

568
00:24:19,230 --> 00:24:21,420
It's not actually the job that's changing,

569
00:24:21,420 --> 00:24:23,580
it's just financial engineering.

570
00:24:23,580 --> 00:24:27,030
So, and oftentimes that comes as well

571
00:24:27,030 --> 00:24:28,770
with a de-skilling of labor.

572
00:24:28,770 --> 00:24:31,080
So, I would argue that there are functional differences,

573
00:24:31,080 --> 00:24:34,590
say with an Uber technology versus a taxi driver technology.

574
00:24:34,590 --> 00:24:37,740
But the basic driver is doing the exact same thing.

575
00:24:37,740 --> 00:24:40,890
The difference is a taxi driver needs to be licensed,

576
00:24:40,890 --> 00:24:43,020
registered, pass tests, et cetera.

577
00:24:43,020 --> 00:24:47,280
Uber was very effective at de-skilling the taxi driver work

578
00:24:47,280 --> 00:24:49,020
where anybody can do that.

579
00:24:49,020 --> 00:24:51,963
You are seeing this now with automation,

580
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:56,940
even just as a lobbying technique with unions

581
00:24:56,940 --> 00:24:59,670
where, well, we don't need you anymore.

582
00:24:59,670 --> 00:25:02,220
You know, don't push too hard on the next union contract,

583
00:25:02,220 --> 00:25:04,560
truck drivers, 'cause we're just gonna automate you out

584
00:25:04,560 --> 00:25:06,720
of existence if you push too hard.

585
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:10,590
Or there are middle grounds there, maybe we'll just descale,

586
00:25:10,590 --> 00:25:12,360
you don't need a CDL anymore.

587
00:25:12,360 --> 00:25:16,200
If you get this level two technology, any random person

588
00:25:16,200 --> 00:25:18,240
with a personal driver's license

589
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:21,180
can drive a truck reasonably safely.

590
00:25:21,180 --> 00:25:24,630
That is the job still exists, but it's now a de-skilled job.

591
00:25:24,630 --> 00:25:26,700
So, these are things I want you to be keeping in mind

592
00:25:26,700 --> 00:25:28,140
as I'm going through

593
